<section class="proof-section" id="proofs" aria-labelledby="proof-heading">
  <div class="section-heading"><h2 id="proof-heading">Three different kinds of evidence</h2><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/danbri/factoidal-core/issues/604">Development record ↗</a></div>
  <div class="evidence-grid">
    <article><span class="evidence-label">KERNEL-CHECKED</span><h3>Pure programs preserve their meaning</h3><p>The typed DSL's fusion and CPU-refinement theorems were compiled by Lean. The theorem hypotheses and primitive interpretations define their scope.</p><a href="https://github.com/danbri/factoidal-core/pull/645">Read the eight checked statements ↗</a></article>
    <article><span class="evidence-label">SHADER MODEL</span><h3>Concrete bytes have modelled behaviour</h3><p>The shader proofs concern parsed source and finite buffers. Each supported GPU fragment has its own admission and buffer requirements.</p><a href="https://github.com/danbri/factoidal-core/issues/646">Read the word-map obligations ↗</a></article>
    <article><span class="evidence-label">LIVE COMPARISON</span><h3>Your two outputs are compared</h3><p>The green result reports an actual byte comparison from this run. It tests the browser, runtime and device path; it does not prove the compiler or driver.</p><a href="#comparison-status">See this run's result ↑</a></article>
  </div>
</section>

<details id="runtime" class="runtime-details">
  <summary>Runtime, provenance and measurement details</summary>
  <p>The worker sends editor bytes to Lean. Lean parses the input and program, builds the typed computation, evaluates the CPU result and produces the execution plan. The page presents those results and submits the selected GPU passes. It contains no JavaScript implementation of the word transformations or stable packing.</p>
  <p>In stable packing, Lean deliberately reorders and pads source storage. The GPU runs the Count, Scan and Scatter sources exported by Lean. Its result comes from the actual terminal offset and payload readback, and every returned byte is compared with the CPU result.</p>
  <p>The word studio's CPU timing includes parsing, checking, evaluation and response encoding. Packing reports preparation and CPU-call times separately. GPU end-to-end time includes transport, command submission, queue execution and readback. These are worker-clock measurements, not isolated device compute times; no speedup ratio is inferred.</p>
  <p>For supported word programs, the plan keeps an intermediate buffer on the GPU between passes. The final comparison also checks the header and spare words against the expected complete buffer. Unsupported programs can still have a CPU result, with an explicit GPU refusal.</p>
  <p>Lean equivalence theorems concern the admitted inputs and formal execution models. Browser shader compilation, the driver, hardware and physical command ordering remain outside those proofs.</p>
  <dl id="runtime-data" hidden></dl>
</details>

<noscript><p>This studio uses a JavaScript worker to run the WebAssembly module and submit GPU commands.</p></noscript>
